+// A document's branch is recorded as a position in the root branch list,
+// and a match reports its version by reading the entry at that same
+// position out of the subrepository the document belongs to. A single
+// input keeps the two lists aligned by construction, because a
+// subrepository's entries are built from the root branch list it was
+// resolved against. Merging inputs under one name breaks that: a
+// subrepository arrives knowing only the branches of the input that
+// contributed it, while the name it lands under can go on to carry the
+// union of every input's branches. Its own documents then sit at
+// positions its metadata never grew an entry for, and are silently
+// dropped from results - or read past the end of the list - depending on
+// where the branch landed in the merged order.
+//
